# Tutorial 03 — Expressions and Characters
# How to declare characters, assign sprites and expressions, and use them in scenes.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_03_expressions
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Hey — see me right now? Smiling, center stage?
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: That's all controlled by the {b}[show]{/b} command and the expression presets defined in your characters file.
Lavender: This lesson covers how to set up characters and make them emote on screen.
{{EXPRESSION_HINT}}

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: What do you want to learn about?
> Declaring characters -> section_declare
> Expressions and presets -> section_expressions
> Showing and hiding characters -> section_show_hide
> Positions and staging -> section_positions
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Declaring Characters ────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_declare
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Before a character can appear on screen, you need to declare them.
Lavender: That happens with {b}@character{/b} — usually in a shared definitions file so every script can use them.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@character lavender "Lavender"{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@character narrator "Narrator"{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The first word after @character is the {b}ID{/b} — what you'll use in scripts. The quoted part is the {b}display name{/b} shown in the text box.
Lavender: For this project, character declarations live in {b}{{CHARACTERS_SCRIPT_PATH}}{/b}.
Lavender: You can add as many characters as you want — side characters, villains, the love interest, whoever.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Just remember: declare once, use everywhere. Don't duplicate declarations across files.
> Next: Expressions -> section_expressions
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Expressions and Presets ─────────────────────────────────────

@label section_expressions
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Once a character is declared, you give them {b}expressions{/b} — the different visual states they can show.
Lavender: There are two ways to do this, depending on your art setup.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}Simple sprites{/b} — one image per expression:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charimg lavender happy assets/characters/sprites/lavender_happy.png{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charimg lavender sad assets/characters/sprites/lavender_sad.png{/color}
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}Layered sprites{/b} — composited from parts (base, eyes, mouth):
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charlayer lavender base assets/.../base.png{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charlayer lavender eyes_neutral assets/.../eyes_neutral.png{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@charpreset lavender happy $base | $eyes_neutral | $mouth_smile{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Layered sprites give you more combinations with fewer art assets. Simple sprites are easier to set up.
Lavender: Either way, the {b}expression name{/b} — "happy", "sad", "talking" — is what you'll use in [show] commands.
> Next: Showing and hiding -> section_show_hide
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Show and Hide ───────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_show_hide
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: To put a character on screen, use {b}[show]{/b}. To remove them, use {b}[hide]{/b}.
Lavender: Let me demonstrate. Watch — I'll step off screen and come back.
[hide lavender]
[wait 600]
narrator: She's gone. The stage is empty.
[wait 400]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: And I'm back! That was {b}[hide lavender]{/b} followed by {b}[show lavender center happy]{/b}.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The show command has three parts:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender center happy]{/color}
narrator: {b}lavender{/b} — character ID.  {b}center{/b} — position.  {b}happy{/b} — expression.
Lavender: You can change expression without moving by keeping the same position:
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Happy!
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: Intense!
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Back to normal. Each [show] is instant — the sprite swaps right away.
> Next: Positions -> section_positions
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Positions ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_positions
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The engine has named stage positions for quick placement.
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: This is {b}left{/b}.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: This is {b}center{/b}.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: This is {b}right{/b}.
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: And this is {b}far_left{/b} — handy when you have several characters on screen.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: For fine control, you can use exact coordinates:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender at 0.70,0.84]{/color}
Lavender: That places me at 70% from the left edge, 84% from the top.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Named positions are easier to read, but coordinates give you pixel-perfect control when you need it.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Try it yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Character and expression cheat sheet:
narrator: {b}@character id "Name"{/b} — declare a character.
narrator: {b}@charimg id expr path{/b} — assign a simple sprite expression.
narrator: {b}@charlayer id part path{/b} — assign a layer part (for compositing).
narrator: {b}@charpreset id expr $part1 | $part2{/b} — define a composited expression.
narrator: {b}[show id position expression]{/b} — display character on screen.
narrator: {b}[hide id]{/b} — remove character from screen.
narrator: {b}Positions:{/b} far_left, left, center, right, far_right, or {b}at X,Y{/b} for exact coords.
narrator: {b}Expressions:{/b} whatever names you define — happy, sad, talking, neutral, etc.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Keep your definitions file organized and name expressions consistently across characters.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Let's see expressions in action. Pick a scene and I'll act it out.
> A greeting -> practice_greeting
> A disagreement -> practice_disagreement
> An awkward moment -> practice_awkward

@label practice_greeting
[hide lavender]
[wait 400]
narrator: The door opened, and there she was.
[show lavender left happy]
Lavender: Oh hey! I wasn't expecting you this early.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Come in, come in! I just made tea.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Notice the flow — hide, narrator beat, then show with a happy expression on the left, slide to center.
[set practice_result 1]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_disagreement
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Look, I hear what you're saying...
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: ...but that's {b}not{/b} what happened and you know it.
[show lavender right emphasis]
narrator: She turned away, arms crossed.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Expression shifts and position changes sell the conflict. No special effects needed.
[set practice_result 2]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_awkward
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: So, um...
[wait 400]
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: ...this is the part where one of us is supposed to say something, right?
[wait 300]
narrator: Neither of them did.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Awkwardness is all about timing and position — small movements, uncomfortable pauses.
[set practice_result 3]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_wrap
[show lavender center happy]
[if practice_result == 1]
Lavender: Warm scenes work best when the character literally moves toward the player — left to center, hide to show.
[elif practice_result == 2]
Lavender: Conflict scenes need strong expression changes. Don't be afraid to let a character look away.
[else]
Lavender: Comedy and awkwardness live in the pauses. The [wait] command is your best friend here.
[endif]
Lavender: Try adding a second character to one of these scenes — that's where staging gets really fun.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_1]
